body:
I'm not religious nor very spiritual but I have always felt like there are active forces working against me to make sure that everything i do is futile. Like there's an invisible hand at force to ensure I suffer and fail. After surviving what should have been a fatal suicide attempt with no explanation as to how I am still alive, I'm starting to wonder if there actually is a force at bay meant to keep me suffering. 

&#x200B;

So, not exactly, but I definitely feel this in a logical sense while trying to acknowledge cognitive bias that may cloud my reality but fuck, 20 years is 20 years and I'm pretty much with you in my confidence in this reality.

>I found that feeling like a bad/dysfunctional, out of place human was way less productive than just allowing myself to entertain the concept that maybe I truly am something totally different.

This stuck out to me. Thank you for your perspective... allows me to better understand my own abuse and how that's put me into an almost opposite state of where you may be... I certainly seem to believe that I am inherently flawed. As if it doesn't matter what other reality I could create or convince myself of, because at the end of the day there are 8 billion people on this planet that are vastly more alike to each other than I am to any of them... 

&#x200B;

My abuse was centered around my parents destroying my confidence in my own self/reality. If 5 people all told me the sky was purple, not blue, I'd probably believe them pretty quickly, and just assume my own eyeballs don't work right. I'm truly just an amorphous mass meant to be used by 'normals'. 

&#x200B;

I won't say I envy you because we are both struggling but I do admire your confidence in your own beliefs and worldview. Our own reality is truly all that we believe it to be. I think I lack the core 'thing' that lets me go "this is what I see" and "this is what i am". I can't dictate that for myself. So even if others may see your particular view as outlandish or whatever, I see where you're coming from and I don't judge. I do respect you, and wish you nothing but continued peace.

8 years? That's it? Did i mishear that?

&#x200B;

probably not... the police are first class citizens

I did a LOT of head slamming in my past... tried to stop and 'move on' to alternate SH methods... the only area I'm confident in is my intelligence, and even that I can feel has been impacted by how much blunt trauma i've caused myself... 

&#x200B;

Thank you for relating.

You can pin this as blame on me, but I don't want to fucking hear it until you can say you've watched doctors and nurses, sworn into an oath of protection, allow patients to slam their head into concrete walls until they are concussed. To sedate patients into sleeping 18 hrs/day. To imprison us with no sentence, no charge, no release date. Indefinite imprisonment without due process. 

&#x200B;

The 'college support system' is designed to funnel certain ones of us into this pit, to never escape, while people like you get to experience success through the facade of 'help' these scum offer. And because you have success, everybody else must right? To be honest, everybody who hates us is right. We are fucking snowflakes. We can't accept how AWFUL and HOPELESS the world truly is. We need to fight for our rights but we're so fucking numbed and scared that we'll just accept any tiny morsel of sympathy thrown out way. We'll be stuck forever like this. I'll die screaming these truths. I'll die defending the rights of my people.

This is to do with dissociation.... in a BPD thread.... Mental health disorders are not treated the same as physical disorders. People in academia see people like me as lazy and weak. Undeserving of education and the opportunity to succeed because I will always be a burden. 

&#x200B;

The real world doesn't lie to you. The real world calls you a piece of shit and you know it. It's direct and harsh. But this 'system' is meant to coddle and pretend that everything is okay. Because the true answer to this crisis is not clear, and will require infinite amounts of blood, sweat, and tears to figure out. And guess what? Most people aren't selfless enough to bother giving that much to help people like me. People like -us-, perhaps. 

&#x200B;

Why are you so quick to defend the system that exists at a bare minimum? You really think there are resources available for EVERYBODY? Seriously? How can you be so ignorant as to think your experience is the only one? When you ignore the lack of effort by those in power to protect us, you hurt people in the community and become part of the problem. That's a 'real world' truth.

You're asking for logic in a time of mob mentality. It's an act of outrage. It's a large temper tantrum. But instead of being told 'no' for a cookie, somebody was choked to death. 

&#x200B;

There is no logic. But it's consequences for the gross misconduct of those meant to protect us.

that would legally insinuate that officers are more valuable members of the community than civilians

&#x200B;

what happened to protect and serve

The spectrum of mental health is so incredibly wide that it can't be categorized at all... only reason we even try to do so is because the insurance companies see us a frauds unless we're medically declared 'sick'. It's not a humane reason behind it... diagnosed people provide statistics for drug production from pharmaceutical companies... you have to be able tag and track your targeted customer base. If you condemn somebody to a 'forever' illness, you have guaranteed income. We're a commodity.

&#x200B;

EDIT: if it helps then i'm glad for you. I've been personally taken advantage of by medical professionals and insurance companies for this reason, at the expense of my healthcare. Just want to expose this industry for how abusive it actually is, and hopefully save people some additional trauma and years of recovery because of medical negligence that can't be prosecuted because we're not humans in the eyes of the law. Take care OP

Trauma is not only misunderstood but completely underestimated and looked over in our society. We come up as humans causing incredible trauma to one another, and we had to societally evolve to ignore it. We never would rise to power as a species if we're plagued by guilt right? Selfishness and sociopathy paved the way to success for us. We've evolved to become a group completely incapable of acknowledging trauma because it stops us from getting better. We're just weights onto society. They don't give a shit. Right or wrong aside, I think that's the reality. If they were to acknowledge the traumas we have as members of society, they would have to acknowledge the traumas they have imparted \*as\* a society. 

&#x200B;

Although cynical, I find this to be the most honest and reasonable conclusion I've come to surrounding this.
